## Statement

Let $W$ be a finite DMC. For each $\rho>0$ and input law $P$, define
$$E_{0,\rm cc}(\rho,P):=\inf_{V:\,V(\cdot\mid x)\ll W(\cdot\mid x)}\left\{\sum_xP(x)D(V(\cdot\mid x)\|W(\cdot\mid x))+\rho I_P(V)\right\},$$
where $V$ ranges over row-stochastic channels on the same alphabets, $I_P(V)$ is the mutual information of $P(x)V(y\mid x)$, and terms with $P(x)=0$ are omitted. At $\rho=0$, set $E_{0,\rm cc}(0,P)=0$. Then
$$\max_P E_{0,\rm cc}(\rho,P)=\max_P E_0(\rho,P),$$
with $E_0$ from [[def-it-gallager-and-expurgated-exponent-functions]]. This equality holds for every $\rho>0$ and is after optimizing over $P$; it is not asserted for each fixed $P$. At $\rho=0$, both sides are zero under the stated conventions.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A finite DMC $W$, a parameter $\rho>0$, and the finite KL and mutual-information conventions.

[F1] [[def-it-one-letter-gallager-exponent-functions]]: $E_0(\rho,P)=-\ln\sum_y(\sum_xP(x)W(y\mid x)^{1/(1+\rho)})^{1+\rho}$.

[F2] [[def-it-finite-mutual-information]]: $I_P(V)$ is $D(PV_{XY}\|P\otimes PV_Y)$.

[F3] [[def-finite-kullback-leibler-divergence]] and [[thm-it-finite-kl-nonnegative-equality]]: finite KL is support-aware, nonnegative, and vanishes exactly at equal probability vectors.

[F4] [[thm-euclidean-heine-borel-pseudocompactness-and-extreme-values]]: every nonempty closed bounded subset of finite-dimensional Euclidean space is compact, and each continuous real-valued function on it attains its minimum and maximum.

[F5] [[def-real-power]] and [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]]: for $t>0$, $t^a=\exp(a\ln t)$ and $(t^a)'=a t^{a-1}$; for $a>0$, the zero-base convention is $0^a=0$.

[F6] [[thm-chain-rule]] and [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]: the scalar chain and product rules apply to the differentiable power and logarithm formulas on positive arguments.

[F7] [[lem-absolute-real-powers-are-convex]]: for every $p\ge1$, $t\mapsto |t|^p$ is continuous and convex on $\mathbb R$.

[F8] [[cor-second-derivative-characterises-convexity]]: a twice-differentiable function on an open interval is convex when its second derivative is nonnegative.

[F9] [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]] and [[thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing]]: $\ln$ and $\exp$ are strictly increasing inverses on their positive domains.

[F10] [[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]]: $\ln'(z)=1/z$ for $z>0$.

## Proof

1.1 Put $\alpha=(1+\rho)^{-1}\in(0,1)$ and, for an output law $Q$, set $Z_x(Q)=\sum_yW(y\mid x)^\alpha Q(y)^{1-\alpha}$. If $Z_x(Q)>0$, define $R_x(y)=W(y\mid x)^\alpha Q(y)^{1-\alpha}/Z_x(Q)$. Expanding the finite sums gives for every $V_x\ll W_x$ with finite $D(V_x\|Q)$ $D(V_x\|W_x)+\rho D(V_x\|Q)=(1+\rho)D(V_x\|R_x)-(1+\rho)\ln Z_x(Q).$ By [F3], the minimum over $V_x$ is $-(1+\rho)\ln Z_x(Q)=-\alpha^{-1}\ln Z_x(Q)$. If $Z_x(Q)=0$, every such objective with positive $P(x)$ is infinite, matching the extended value of this expression. [F1, F3, given, algebra, cases]

1.2 Define $S_y(P)=\sum_xP(x)W(y\mid x)^\alpha$ and $\Psi(P)=\sum_yS_y(P)^{1/\alpha}$. Then [F1] gives $E_0(\rho,P)=-\ln\Psi(P)$. Since $1/\alpha=1+\rho>1$, [F7] shows that each summand $S_y(P)^{1/\alpha}$ is continuous and convex as a function of $P$; hence $\Psi$ is continuous and convex on the input simplex. The simplex is nonempty because it contains a point mass, closed as the intersection of finitely many coordinate half-spaces with the affine equation $\sum_xP(x)=1$, and bounded because every coordinate lies in $[0,1]$. By [F4] it is compact, so $\Psi$ attains a minimum at some $P^*$. For every input law at least one coordinate of $P$ is positive, and that row of $W$ has a positive entry; hence at least one $S_y(P)>0$ and $\Psi(P)>0$. Also, for every $r>0$, $t^r\to0$ as $t\downarrow0$: given $\varepsilon>0$, take $\delta=\varepsilon^{1/r}$; if $0<t<\delta$, strict monotonicity of $\ln$ and $\exp$ gives $t^r<\varepsilon$. Write $S_y=S_y(P^*)$, $\Psi_* =\Psi(P^*)>0$, $T=\{y:S_y>0\}$, and $q^*(y)=S_y^{1/\alpha}/\Psi_*$ on $T$, with $q^*(y)=0$ outside $T$. [F1, F4, F7, F9, given, algebra]

2.1 For any $V$ and output law $Q$, expansion of finite KL gives $\sum_xP(x)D(V_x\|Q)=I_P(V)+D(PV_Y\|Q)\ge I_P(V),$ with equality at $Q=PV_Y$ by [F2–F3]. Taking the joint infimum over $V,Q$ and applying step 1.1 row by row therefore yields $E_{0,\rm cc}(\rho,P)=\inf_Q\sum_{x:P(x)>0}P(x)f_x(Q),\quad f_x(Q):=-\alpha^{-1}\ln Z_x(Q),$ with $f_x(Q)=+\infty$ when $Z_x(Q)=0.$ Coordinates with $P(x)=0$ are omitted, so no $0\cdot\infty$ term is used. [F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]

2.2 Put $T_x=\sum_yW(y\mid x)^\alpha S_y^\rho$. For each $x$, move from $P^*$ toward the point mass $\delta_x$ along $P_t=(1-t)P^*+t\delta_x$, so $S_y(P_t)=S_y+t(W(y\mid x)^\alpha-S_y)$. If $S_y>0$, [F5]–[F6] give the derivative of $S_y(P_t)^{1/\alpha}$ at $t=0$ as $\alpha^{-1}S_y^\rho(W(y\mid x)^\alpha-S_y)$. If $S_y=0$, the term equals $t^{1/\alpha}W(y\mid x)$, whose right derivative at zero is zero because $1/\alpha-1=\rho>0$ and the endpoint limit in step 1.2 applies. Therefore the right derivative of $\Psi(P_t)$ at zero is $\alpha^{-1}(T_x-\Psi_*)$, using $\sum_yS_y^{1/\alpha}=\Psi_*$. The minimum at $t=0$ makes this right derivative nonnegative, so $T_x\ge\Psi_*$. Also $\sum_xP^*(x)T_x=\sum_yS_y^{1+\rho}=\Psi_*$. Since every $T_x\ge\Psi_*$ and this weighted average equals $\Psi_*$, equality holds whenever $P^*(x)>0$. Substitution into $Z_x(q^*)=\Psi_*^{-(1-\alpha)}T_x$ gives $Z_x(q^*)\ge\Psi_*^\alpha$, with equality for every active input. Thus $f_x(q^*)\le-\ln\Psi_*$ for all $x$, with equality on $\operatorname{supp}P^*$. [F1, F5, F6, step 1.2, algebra]

3.1 Applying step 2.1 with the particular output law $q^*$ gives, for every $P$, $E_{0,\rm cc}(\rho,P)\le\sum_xP(x)f_x(q^*)\le-\ln\Psi_* =\max_{P'}E_0(\rho,P').$ Therefore $\max_PE_{0,\rm cc}(\rho,P)\le\max_PE_0(\rho,P)$. [step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra]

3.2 On $[0,\infty)$, the power $t^{1-\alpha}$ is continuous by the endpoint estimate in step 1.2 and concave: on $(0,\infty)$ the second derivative of $-t^{1-\alpha}$ is $(1-\alpha)\alpha t^{-1-\alpha}>0$, so [F5], [F6], and [F8] make $-t^{1-\alpha}$ convex there; continuity extends the concavity inequality to zero. Thus each $Z_x(Q)$ is concave. The function $-\ln z$ is decreasing and convex on $(0,\infty)$, since $\ln$ is increasing by [F9] and $(-\ln z)''=z^{-2}>0$ by [F5], [F6], [F8], and [F10]. Consequently $f_x(Q)=-\alpha^{-1}\ln Z_x(Q)$ is convex wherever $Z_x(Q)>0$: concavity of $Z_x$, monotonicity of $-\ln$, and convexity of $-\ln$ give the convex-combination inequality directly. Hence $F(Q)=\sum_{x:P^*(x)>0}P^*(x)f_x(Q)$ is convex on its finite domain. For $y\in T$, differentiating at the positive point $q^*$ and using $Z_x(q^*)=\Psi_*^\alpha$ for active $x$ from step 2.2 gives $$\frac{\partial F}{\partial Q(y)}(q^*)=-\frac{1-\alpha}{\alpha}\frac{\Psi_*^\alpha}{S_y}\frac{\sum_{x:P^*(x)>0}P^*(x)W(y\mid x)^\alpha}{\Psi_*^\alpha}=-\rho.$$ For any strictly positive probability vector $Q$ on $T$, let $g(t)=F((1-t)q^*+tQ)$. It is convex, and $g'_+(0)=\sum_{y\in T}(-\rho)(Q(y)-q^*(y))=0$. Convexity gives $(g(t)-g(0))/t\le g(1)-g(0)$ for $0<t<1$; letting $t\downarrow0$ yields $F(Q)\ge F(q^*)$. If $Q$ has zero coordinates on $T$ but $F(Q)<\infty$, then every active $Z_x(Q)>0$; apply the result to $Q_t=(1-t)Q+tq^*$ and let $t\downarrow0$, using continuity of the finite sums and logarithms on positive $Z_x(Q)$. If $F(Q)=+\infty$, the inequality is immediate. Thus $F(Q)\ge F(q^*)$ for every probability vector supported on $T$. [F5, F6, F8, F9, F10, step 1.2, step 2.2]

4.1 For a general output law $Q$, let $c=Q(T)$. If $c=0$, every active row has $Z_x(Q)=0$ and $F(Q)=+\infty$. If $c>0$, normalize $Q_T=Q(\cdot\mid T)$. Every active row is supported on $T$, so $Z_x(Q)=c^{1-\alpha}Z_x(Q_T)$ and $f_x(Q)=f_x(Q_T)-\rho\ln c\ge f_x(Q_T)$. Step 3.2 then gives $F(Q)\ge F(q^*)$. Since active inputs satisfy $f_x(q^*)=-\ln\Psi_*$, step 2.1 yields $E_{0,\rm cc}(\rho,P^*)=F(q^*)=-\ln\Psi_*=\max_PE_0(\rho,P)$. [step 2.2, step 3.2, cases, algebra]

5.1 Step 4.1 gives $\max_PE_{0,\rm cc}(\rho,P)\ge E_{0,\rm cc}(\rho,P^*)=\max_PE_0(\rho,P)$, while step 3.1 gives the reverse inequality. This proves the globally optimized identity and establishes no pointwise-in-$P$ equality. [step 3.1, step 4.1, algebra] ∎
